Serialize loading, unloading and resetting the crypto providers

Applies to picoquic 36f49b73d532369882d97474ac018655b6739a58, after 0001 to
0010.

picoquic_create() loads the crypto providers on first use through
picoquic_tls_api_init(), which tests tls_api_is_init without synchronization,
then clears the provider tables (picoquic_tls_api_zero) and registers each
provider into them. Two contexts created at the same time on different threads
both pass that test: one thread clears the cipher-suite and key-exchange tables
and the provider function pointers while the other is registering into them or
already reading them. ThreadSanitizer reports those pairs between
picoquic_tls_api_zero(), picoquic_register_ciphersuite(),
picoquic_register_key_exchange_algorithm() and picoquic_create_ptls_context(),
and a context can be left without a cipher suite or with a NULL crypto random
provider. picoquic_init_openssl() has the same shape: it sets openssl_is_init
before the default OpenSSL provider is loaded.

One statically initialized lock (a pthread mutex, or an SRW lock on Windows)
now makes picoquic_tls_api_init(), picoquic_tls_api_unload() and
picoquic_tls_api_reset() each atomic, so concurrent first use loads the
providers once and every context sees them complete; the OpenSSL provider load
runs inside it. Provider selection, the registered tables and every API are
unchanged. The lock does not make an unload or a reset safe while a context
exists or is being created: those still require that no other picoquic call is
in progress.

Upstream: the 2026-11-12 release (v2026.11.12.36f49b7) still loads the
providers without synchronization.

Test: tls_api_concurrent_init (tls_api_test.c) unloads the providers and
creates contexts on eight threads released together; each must create its
context and see exactly the tables a serial initialization produces. After a
reset that selects other providers (no OpenSSL), made while no context exists,
contexts created concurrently must see that selection rather than a reload.
The test leaves the default providers loaded. Without the lock it fails, and
under ThreadSanitizer it reports the races; with the lock it passes and
ThreadSanitizer reports none.

